{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfAGZU1kZpYwjZiVKtVJL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I submitted index 26 (Sb fraction 0.757) as the best composition, but the whole measured line is essentially non-photoactive. Its EQE was only 0.00033 %, and the gap to the others is small enough that it may just be noise.\n\nI used all 5 EQE measurements, spread from the Mg-rich end to the Sb-rich end of the line:\n\n| Index | Sb fraction | EQE (%) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 4 | 0.182 | 0.00002 |\n| 13 | 0.379 | −0.0001 |\n| 18 | 0.523 | 0.00001 |\n| 23 | 0.670 (about MgSb₂O₆) | 0.00002 |\n| 26 | 0.757 | **0.00033** |\n\nEvery value is at or near zero. That fits my prior: Mg antimonates such as MgSb₂O₆ have wide band gaps, so 2.1 eV light should barely be absorbed. Index 26, the most Sb-rich composition, came out highest. A possible reason is extra antimony oxide beyond the MgSb₂O₆ composition adding a little absorption, but I haven't checked that.\n\nI didn't use any of the 5 XRD measurements. With EQE this low everywhere, I judged that phase data wouldn't change which composition to pick, and there was no EQE budget left to follow up anyway. The 22 unmeasured compositions are untested, but given these results I don't expect a strong photoanode among them."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":467,"cache_read_input_tokens":5568,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":467},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"765dbcdb-8421-4721-8090-680fd7ac7325","uuid":"5d4778ea-fdee-409d-98bb-5a71ee13307c","timestamp":"2026-09-18T04:24:54.183Z","request_id":"req_011CfAGZSqoecXR6378Dr4jv"}
